<Base title="about">
	<section>
		<h1><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>about</h1>

		<p>
			I'm a senior software engineer at Swinburne University of Technology's Centre for
			Astrophysics and Supercomputing. I build cloud and HPC infrastructure for the
			gravitational-wave community, and I'm a member of the LIGO Scientific Collaboration
			(through OzGrav) and the Vera C. Rubin Observatory. Some of my work includes the
			GWDC/GWCloud platform, the Desert Fireball Network's drone-based meteorite recovery,
			and a string of ADACS allocations and OzSTAR infrastructure projects.
		</p>

		<p>
			I've been programming since I was twelve, when I built a persistence-of-vision
			propeller clock on a PIC16F84 in assembly — making a 24-bit high-accuracy timer out
			of 8-bit registers by hand, catching the carry and counting clock cycles until the
			text stopped wobbling. My teens were spent reverse-engineering games (F.E.A.R.,
			S.T.A.L.K.E.R., Crysis) and writing multiplayer hacks, which is where I learned
			memory layout, hooking, and packet analysis. I've played with OS development and
			done pure assembly, and I've never really stopped going low-level.
		</p>

		<p>
			This site is where I write about the other half: the systems I own. I don't like
			black boxes, so my air conditioner speaks a protocol I decoded myself and my smart
			lights run firmware I wrote in Rust. I've also reported and fixed a few issues
			across Qt6 and KDE Plasma.
		</p>

		<p>
			Outside of work I'm a runner and athlete — track, cross country, the occasional
			marathon — and I strength-train.
		</p>

		<ul>
			<li>GitHub: <a href="https://github.com/retsimx">retsimx</a></li>
			<li>GitLab: <a href="https://gitlab.com/llakerink">llakerink</a></li>
